From c7abc51a6b20e1c446b76901ec673f1ac8f876cb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: T0 <dev@local>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 15:30:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] T5: transfer and disposal orchestration services

ITransferService/TransferService and IDisposalService/DisposalService for
API 4.10, wired into ServiceManager and DI. No controllers yet (T6).

Settlement rule (documented once on TransferService, applied per line):
create moves the full quantity from source Available into source Hold.
Settlement always releases the full quantity from source Hold regardless
of split, adds the received portion to target Available, and - if
anything is left over - adds it to target Hold and carries it on an
auto-generated return transfer, which settles through the exact same path
recursively. Disposed quantity touches no further stock: it already left
source Hold and never entered target Available.

TransferService.CreateAsync: validates both branches (inactive target
blocks, inactive source doesn't - EC-04) and every product line before
writing anything; selects Known-way cards by explicit id, Unknown-way by
FIFO; one DB transaction for the whole request.

TransferService.ReceiveAsync/DisposeAsync share one settlement path.
Receive carries a full per-product disposition (received/disposed, with
the remainder derived, never supplied); Dispose is receive with every
line's full quantity marked disposed. Both go through the same
SettleAsync, so a return transfer settles exactly like any other transfer.

DisposalService.CreateAsync: standalone write-off of cards sitting at a
branch (Available/FailedPrinting/Expired), named by id or FIFO quantity.
Rejects system admin outright - no read-only path for this one, unlike
transfers.

Corrections made during this phase, each explained where it lives:
- CardTransfer.ParentTransferId: chains are not capped at one level: a
  return settles through the same disposition model as any transfer, so
  a partial receipt on a return can spawn a further return.
- TransferErrors.ReturnMustBeSettledInFull removed (and its resx keys)
  superseded by the above - no longer a real restriction.
- TransferService's stock-rule doc corrects Addendum A 2.2: the Hold
  release on disposal is always the line's own source, for the full
  transacted quantity in one release - not a decrement keyed to whichever
  branch performs the write-off.
- CardTransferItem (T1 doc comment) corrected in T3: rows exist for
  Unknown-way lines too, system-selected, never caller-visible.

Bugs caught in review before commit, not after:
- SettleAsync originally returned a value-DTO built inside the transaction
  lambda, capturing returnTransfer.Id/disposal.Id as 0 (unset until
  SaveChanges runs, which happens after the lambda returns). Fixed by
  lifting those references to outer scope and building the result after
  commit - the same pattern BatchUploadService already uses for its own
  counters.
- The Hold-release-underrun check reused StockErrors.InsufficientAvailable,
  whose message is specifically about Available going negative. Replaced
  with TransferErrors.StockInconsistency, matching what the condition
  actually represents (a should-be-unreachable bookkeeping divergence).
- DisposalService leaked TransferErrors.NotFound/ItemNotFound/
  PersistenceConflict to its own callers. Added DisposalErrors.NotFound/
  CardNotFound/PersistenceConflict and fixed the three call sites - a
  disposal-endpoint caller has no reason to see a Transfer.* code.

ICardTransferRepo.GetForUpdateAsync now includes Items.ProductItem
(T3 gap): settlement needs it to group items by product line and to
mutate card Status/BranchID without a second round trip.

CardDisposalRepo.GetPagedAsync now includes Items (T3 gap): the list
projection's CardCount was silently always zero without it.

IAuditLogger gained StageAction: CardTransfer/CardTransferProduct/
CardTransferItem/CardDisposal/CardDisposalItem deliberately don't derive
AuditableEntity (append-only, ERD 6.5), so the interceptor never sees
them and these actions had no audit trail at all otherwise. Staged, not
saved, inside the method - committed via a second SaveChanges call after
the entity's own transaction, since the entity's correctness never
depends on the audit row.

90 localized keys total across both languages, cross-checked: every error
code has a key in both files, no orphans, every {0} matches a WithArg call.
